{"abstract":"Embedded software is growing fast in size and complexity, leading to intimate mixture of complex architectures and complex control. Consequently, software speci cation requires modeling both structures and behaviour of systems. Unfortunately, existing languages do not integrate these aspects well, usually prioritizing one of them. It is common to develop a separate language for each of these facets In this paper, we contribute Clafer: a small language that attempts to tackle this challenge. It combines rich structural modeling with state of the art behavioural formalisms. We are not aware of any other modeling language that seamlessly combines these facets common to system and software modeling. We show how Clafer, in a single unified syntax and semantics, allows capturing feature models (variability), component models, discrete control models (automata) and variability encompassing all these aspects. The language is built on top of first order logic with quantifiers over basic entities (for modeling structures) combined with linear temporal logic (for modeling behaviour). On top of this semantic foundation we build a simple but expressive syntax, enriched with carefully selected syntactic expansions that cover hierarchical modeling, associations, automata, scenarios, and Dwyer’s property patterns. We evaluate Clafer using a power window case study, and comparing it against other notations that substantially overlap with its scope (SysML, AADL, Temporal OCL and Live Sequence Charts), discussing benefits and perils of using a single notation for the purpose.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Juodisius, Paulius","Sarkar, Atrisha","Mukkamala, Raghava Rao","Antkiewicz, Michal","Czarnecki, Krzysztof","Wasowski, Andrzej"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383074"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Canada","Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Kristiania University College","University of Waterloo"],"keyword":["Behaviour modeling","Feature Modeling","Language Design","Semantics","Variability Modeling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"clafer lightweight modeling of structure behaviour and variability","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.22152/programming-journal.org/2019/3/2","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383074","identifier":[{"id":"85076044712","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7ae8926b-5bda-4642-bf55-6eef2e0a631c"],"keyword":["Behaviour modeling","Feature Modeling","Language Design","Semantics","Variability Modeling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3D59BF12F1C9BE0B9893F58BF6305400"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.22152/programming-journal.org/2019/3/2","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83703690/1807.08576.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ca","key":"university of waterloo","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Waterloo","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"kristiania university college","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Kristiania University College","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT Unive